    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of *every* layer, so all devices participate in every layer's computation.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts* (whole FFN blocks), while attention and other dense layers are typically replicated (or sharded via TP/DP separately).\n\nIn practice, production systems combine them: dense layers use TP, MoE layers use EP, and data parallelism (DP) may sit on top for throughput. This note covers the mechanics of both, their data flow, the collectives involved, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer with:\n\n- Attention: $Y = \\text{softmax}(QK^T/\\sqrt{d})V \\cdot W_O$\n- MLP: $Y = \\text{act}(X W_1) W_2$\n\nThe canonical Megatron-style sharding:\n\n| Component | Sharding | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| $W_Q, W_K, W_V$ | Column-parallel (split output dim) | Attention heads are independent; splitting output dim splits heads across devices |\n| $W_O$ | Row-parallel (split input dim) | Partial products sum back to full output |\n| $W_1$ (up/gate proj) | Column-parallel | Activation is elementwise, so splitting hidden dim is safe |\n| $W_2$ (down proj) | Row",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
